Goes wrong with 
{def apply[AA,B](f: AA => B)(x: AA) = f(x); apply isEmpty [3]}

Evaluation on "apply isEmpty [3]".  

Calls the FunctionValue associated with "apply isEmpty" to argument "[3]".

The formal type parameters AA and B were previously bound, so we shouldn't be
trying to bind them again.

..

Goes wrong with 

{def apply[AA,B](f: AA => B)(x: AA) = f(x); 
 def g[A <: Eq](x: A) = x == x; apply g 4}

Type checking "apply g 4".  "apply g" has type parameter A <: Eq.  So the application's formal type parameters are set to this.  ** This looks wrong **. 

Evaluation on "apply g"

Calls the FunctionValue associated with "f(x)" with argument a FunctionValue
(presumably g), aTParams = List(TypeVar(2), BoolType)

Evaluation on "apply g 4", atps = List(IntType) ** looks wrong **


